{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-162583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=4,$ $y=-1,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=({x}^{2}+y)\\,i-yz\\,j+4xz\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4xz) \\\\  & =2x-z+4x \\\\  & =6x-z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(6x-z)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,6x-z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ad225393ed3a496c54e484b4d782296fc695fc8e88abb50104d89bcf3f5f67"} {"question" "q-162583" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162583" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x+3z"] ["math" "2x-y-2z"] ["math" "2x-z+4"] ["math" "6x+4z"] ["math" "6x-z"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162583" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6x-z"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and $y=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=({y}^{3}-z)\\,i+z\\,j+2xyz\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xyz) \\\\  & =0+0+2xy \\\\  & =2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}2xy\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type III solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2xy\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}^{2}\\,2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ad225393ed3a496c54e484b4d782296fc695fc8e88abb50104d89bcf3f5f67"} {"question" "q-162589" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162589" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,x+y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2}^{{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,3{y}^{2}+xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}^{2}\\,2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}^{2}\\,3{y}^{2}+xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162589" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}^{2}\\,2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=0,$ $y=1,$ $y=2,$ $z=0,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=xy\\,i+2y\\,j+{x}^{2}zk,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =y+2+{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+y+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}({x}^{2}+y+2)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,{x}^{2}+y+2\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ad225393ed3a496c54e484b4d782296fc695fc8e88abb50104d89bcf3f5f67"} {"question" "q-162581" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162581" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x+y+1"] ["math" "xy+2"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+2y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+xy"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+y+2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162581" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}+y+2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162408" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y=-1,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $x=y-2z$ and $x=4-{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=x{y}^{2}\\,i+xz\\,j+y\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162408" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+0+0 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type II solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,y-2z\\le x\\le 4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162408" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ad225393ed3a496c54e484b4d782296fc695fc8e88abb50104d89bcf3f5f67"} {"question" "q-162408" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162408" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\,z+3y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}^{y-2z}\\,2y+1\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}^{y-2z}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,2y+1\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162408" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z={x}^{2}+y.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(x+y)\\,i-2y\\,j+{x}^{2}z\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =1+(-2)+{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}({x}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le z\\le {x}^{2}+y}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ad225393ed3a496c54e484b4d782296fc695fc8e88abb50104d89bcf3f5f67"} {"question" "q-162588" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162588" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2y}^{2{x}^{2}+1}\\,y+z-2\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2y}^{2{x}^{2}+1}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,2xz\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,y+z-2\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162588" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/assets/581ac462bfcfead22a6c5ad4eb247f05adb1a677e92cac51954b0746cd083ab7.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨x-y,\\,y+z,\\,2x-3z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $3$ whose base is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2x-3z) \\\\  & =1+1+(-3) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid cylinder enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}-1\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =-{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =-\\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =-\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-12\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ad225393ed3a496c54e484b4d782296fc695fc8e88abb50104d89bcf3f5f67"} {"question" "q-162569" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162569" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-12\\pi "] ["math" "-16\\pi "] ["math" "-8\\pi "] ["math" "4\\pi "] ["math" "6\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162569" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-12\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/assets/aac27058402f89bef73700129a7812b3cf4f971c11975fc1711a46b8d10e61fe.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨3x+y{e}^{z},\\,xy+2,\\,1-xz⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x+y{e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+2)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(1-xz) \\\\  & =3+x+(-x) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the rectangular solid enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ad225393ed3a496c54e484b4d782296fc695fc8e88abb50104d89bcf3f5f67"} {"question" "q-162572" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162572" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "18"] ["math" "21"] ["math" "24"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162572" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "18"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by $(x+2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ and the vector field $F=({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,i+4y\\,j+2xz\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xz) \\\\  & =-2x+4+2x \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $1$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ad225393ed3a496c54e484b4d782296fc695fc8e88abb50104d89bcf3f5f67"} {"question" "q-162580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8