here of radius $2$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}9\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =9{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =96\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ad225393ed3a496c54e484b4d782296fc695fc8e88abb50104d89bcf3f5f67"} {"question" "q-162578" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162578" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "64\\pi "] ["math" "78\\pi "] ["math" "96\\pi "] ["math" "\\frac{128\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{256\\pi }{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162578" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "96\\pi "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=25$ and the vector field $F=x\\,i+xz\\,j+3z\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3z) \\\\  & =1+0+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $5$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {5}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\,000\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ad225393ed3a496c54e484b4d782296fc695fc8e88abb50104d89bcf3f5f67"} {"question" "q-162410" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162410" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{2\\,000\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{400\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{800\\pi }{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162410" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2\\,000\\pi }{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,j+(x+y)\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+y) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+0+0 \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡z]_{z=0}^{z=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =({2}^{2}-{0}^{2})\\cdot 1\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ad225393ed3a496c54e484b4d782296fc695fc8e88abb50104d89bcf3f5f67"} {"question" "q-162606" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162606" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162606" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=1,$ $x=e,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=0,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,i+{z}^{2}\\,j+(x-{y}^{3})\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x-{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}+0+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{x=1}^{x=e}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\cdot [z]_{z=0}^{z=2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\cdot (\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2})\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ad225393ed3a496c54e484b4d782296fc695fc8e88abb50104d89bcf3f5f67"} {"question" "q-162596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162594" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=4,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}y\\,i+2z\\,j+xyz\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162594" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xyz) \\\\  & =2xy+0+xy \\\\  & =3xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3xy\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}3xy\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}3\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=4}\\cdot [3z]_{z=1}^{z=2} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (8-0)\\cdot (6-3) \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162594" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ad225393ed3a496c54e484b4d782296fc695fc8e88abb50104d89bcf3f5f67"} {"question" "q-162594" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162594" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "28"] ["math" "36"] ["math" "48"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162594" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "48"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=1,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=3.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}\\,i+2y\\,j+(x+{y}^{2})\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2x+2+0 \\\\  & =2x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(2x+2)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(2x+2)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}+2x]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}+2(1)-0)\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ad225393ed3a496c54e484b4d782296fc695fc8e88abb50104d89bcf3f5f67"} {"question" "q-162599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=1,$ $y=2,$ $z=-1,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=2y\\,i+x{y}^{2}\\,j+2xyz\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xyz) \\\\  & =0+2xy+2xy \\\\  & =4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4xy\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}4xy\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=1}^{y=2}\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (4-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ad225393ed3a496c54e484b4d782296fc695fc8e88abb50104d89bcf3f5f67"} {"question" "q-162600" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162600" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "q-162600" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f783f2845661afa12ec7b659d7e3053be80c8ef344d55d4748b6e1027a03e6e8"} {"question" "e-12447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z=1+x^2+y^2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} = (x+z )\\, \\mathbf{i} + yz \\, \\mathbf{j} + x^2y \\, \\mathbf{k},$ express the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ as a triple integral, and then as a repeated integral." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/example-12447.json" "file_sha256" "9d5c1841951e14a457d07604bc6f13795412d89ef969002dff3a1dddd03e25be"} {"question" "e-12447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =1+z+0 \\\\  & =z+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iiint\\limits_R (z+1) \\: \\textrm{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\:-1 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\: y \\leq z \\leq 1+x^2+y^2 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nExpressing our triple integral as a repeated integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z+1\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,z+1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/example-12447.json" "file_sha256" "9d5c1841951e14a457d07604bc6f13795412d89ef969002dff3a1dddd03e25be"} {"question" "e-12448" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the positively oriented surface $S$ given by $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 4$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = 2x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{k}.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/example-12448.json" "file_sha256" "dfb0995bd3f54cac0488160bf9443e2fd9bdd01ae0ab9a32e6843ccd10912d91"} {"question" "e-12448" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4) \\\\  & =2+1+0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $2$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =32\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/example-12448.json" "file_sha256" "dfb0995bd3f54cac0488160bf9443e2fd9bdd01ae0ab9a32e6843ccd10912d91"} {"question" "e-12449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} =e^z \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2y^2 \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4x^2yz \\, \\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/example-12449.json" "file_sha256" "e11b25e959d94d50de7ebdd9dcd6c0468b6438887501e0468c16ae0f8f0194c1"} {"question" "e-12449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{x}^{2}yz) \\\\  & =0+2{x}^{2}y+4{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (4-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/example-12449.json" "file_sha256" "e11b25e959d94d50de7ebdd9dcd6c0468b6438887501e0468c16ae0f8f0194c1"} {"question" "q-162408" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "ed0cef56540095fb861b93f365f189a4090a219a2af8166ad7d7aa30dd632f83" "reviewer_session" "01a11908-fa8d-7c61-8e03-c1185d0b9506" "rationale" "For F=(xy²,xz,y), divergence is y²+0+0=y². Outward orientation permits the divergence theorem. The solid has -1≤y≤1, 0≤z≤1, and y-2z≤x≤4-y²-z², giving exactly original option 3. The authentic worked solution confirms these bounds and powers, and saved grading reports Correct. This is a derived mathematical judgment. The malformed stored serialization does not justify globally interpreting adjacent digits as exponents." "bindings" {"problem" "5efa176577a13ca441fc4099670952bee96ef0b60a04c492d9aeb5315d714bcd" "worked_solution" "fc32b9dff7cb6e6948becd78bafcaad81f06dfbf4cbee03082d0a3078bb31977" "original_field" "5a